      "text": "To solve this problem, we need to determine the final price of the shirt after applying the two successive discounts and then find a single discount that gives the same final price.\n\nFirst, let's calculate the sale price of the shirt after the initial 20% discount.\n\nThe regular price of the shirt is $30. A 20% discount means the shirt is sold for 80% of its original price.\n\\[\n\\text{Sale price} = 30 \\times (1 - 0.20) = 30 \\times 0.80 = 24\n\\]\nSo, the sale price of the shirt is $24.\n\nNext, Mary has a coupon that offers an additional 25% off the sale price. A 25% discount means the shirt is sold for 75% of the sale price.\n\\[\n\\text{Final price after coupon} = 24 \\times (1 - 0.25) = 24 \\times 0.75 = 18\n\\]\nSo, the final price of the shirt after applying the coupon is $18.\n\nNow, we need to find a single percent discount that would give the same final price as the two successive discounts. Let the single discount be \\( x \\). The final price after a single \\( x \\)% discount on the original price of $30 is:\n\\[\n\\text{Final price} = 30 \\times (1 - \\frac{x}{100})\n\\]\nWe know this final price is $18, so we set up the equation:\n\\[\n30 \\times (1 - \\frac{x}{100}) = 18\n\\]\nSolving for \\( x \\):\n\\[\n1 - \\frac{x}{100} = \\frac{18}{30} = 0.6\n\\]\n\\[\n\\frac{x}{100} = 1 - 0.6 = 0.4\n\\]\n\\[\nx = 0.4 \\times 100 = 40\n\\]\nTherefore, the single percent discount that would give the same final price as the two successive discounts is \\(\\boxed{40}\\).",
