      "text": "To solve the problem, we need to determine the final price of the shirt after applying both successive discounts and then find the single equivalent discount that would yield the same final price.\n\n1. **Calculate the sale price after the first discount:**\n   The shirt has an initial price of \\( \\$30 \\) and is on sale for \\( 20\\% \\) off.\n   \\[\n   \\text{Discount amount} = 20\\% \\times 30 = 0.20 \\times 30 = 6\n   \\]\n   \\[\n   \\text{Sale price} = 30 - 6 = 24\n   \\]\n\n2. **Calculate the price after applying the second discount:**\n   The sale price of \\( \\$24 \\) is now subject to a \\( 25\\% \\) coupon discount.\n   \\[\n   \\text{Discount amount} = 25\\% \\times 24 = 0.25 \\times 24 = 6\n   \\]\n   \\[\n   \\text{Final price} = 24 - 6 = 18\n   \\]\n\n3. **Determine the single equivalent discount:**\n   We need to find the single discount that would reduce the original price (\\( \\$30 \\)) to the final price (\\( \\$18 \\)).\n   \\[\n   \\text{Original price} = \\$30\n   \\]\n   \\[\n   \\text{Final price} = \\$18\n   \\]\n   \\[\n   \\text{Discount amount} = \\$30 - \\$18 = \\$12\n   \\]\n   \\[\n   \\text{Single equivalent discount} = \\frac{\\$12}{\\$30} \\times 100\\% = 40\\%\n   \\]\n\nThus, the single percent discount that would give the same final price as the two successive discounts is \\(\\boxed{40}\\).",
