      "sample_index": 2,
      "text": "To determine the final price of the shirt after applying the two successive discounts, we can follow these steps:\n\n1. **Apply the first discount (20%) to the regular price:**\n   The regular price of the shirt is $30.\n   The amount of the first discount is \\(20\\% \\times 30 = 0.20 \\times 30 = 6\\) dollars.\n   Therefore, the sale price after the first discount is \\(30 - 6 = 24\\) dollars.\n\n2. **Apply the second discount (25%) to the sale price:**\n   The sale price after the first discount is $24.\n   The amount of the second discount is \\(25\\% \\times 24 = 0.25 \\times 24 = 6\\) dollars.\n   Therefore, the final price after both discounts is \\(24 - 6 = 18\\) dollars.\n\n3. **Determine the single percent discount that would result in the same final price:**\n   We need to find the single discount \\(D\\) such that \\(30 \\times (1 - D) = 18\\).\n   Solving for \\(D\\):\n   \\[\n   30 \\times (1 - D) = 18\n   \\]\n   Dividing both sides by 30:\n   \\[\n   1 - D = \\frac{18}{30} = 0.6\n   \\]\n   Solving for \\(D\\):\n   \\[\n   D = 1 - 0.6 = 0.4\n   \\]\n   The single percent discount is \\(0.4 \\times 100\\% = 40\\%\\).\n\nTherefore, the single percent discount that would give the same final price as the two successive discounts is \\(\\boxed{40}\\).",
