      "sample_index": 3,
      "text": "To solve the problem, we need to determine the final price of the shirt after applying both successive discounts and then find the single percentage equivalent to those discounts.\n\n1. **Calculate the sale price after the first discount:**\n   The original price of the shirt is $30. The first discount is 20% off.\n   \\[\n   \\text{Discount amount} = 30 \\times \\frac{20}{100} = 30 \\times 0.20 = 6\n   \\]\n   \\[\n   \\text{Sale price} = 30 - 6 = 24\n   \\]\n\n2. **Calculate the final price after applying the second discount:**\n   The second discount is 25% off the sale price of $24.\n   \\[\n   \\text{Discount amount} = 24 \\times \\frac{25}{100} = 24 \\times 0.25 = 6\n   \\]\n   \\[\n   \\text{Final price} = 24 - 6 = 18\n   \\]\n\n3. **Determine the final price as a single percentage discount:**\n   The final price after both discounts is $18. The original price was $30. The difference between the original price and the final price is:\n   \\[\n   30 - 18 = 12\n   \\]\n   The single percentage discount is the fraction of the original price that the final price represents, multiplied by 100.\n   \\[\n   \\text{Single percentage discount} = \\left( \\frac{12}{30} \\right) \\times 100 = 0.40 \\times 100 = 40\\%\n   \\]\n\nTherefore, the single percent discount that would give the same final price as the two successive discounts is \\(\\boxed{40}\\).",
